from .lg import S

PROBLEMS = [
 (39, ("(Medium, JEE Advanced style, integer) (<i>S</i>)-2-Methylbutanoic acid is treated with (i) SOCl<sub>2</sub>; (ii) NaN<sub>3</sub>; (iii) toluene, 80 °C; (iv) aqueous HCl, heat; (v) NaOH. Let <i>x</i> be the number of carbon atoms in the final organic product and <i>y</i> the number of moles of gas evolved per mole of acid in steps (iii)–(iv). Give <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> and the CIP descriptor of the product.",
      S([("(S)-acid", "CC[C@H](C)C(=O)O")]),
      "6 ; (S)-butan-2-amine",
      "Acid chloride, acyl azide, then thermal Curtius rearrangement: R migrates from C to N with loss of N<sub>2</sub> (1 mol) giving the isocyanate; acid hydrolysis of RNCO gives a carbamic acid that loses CO<sub>2</sub> (1 mol), so y = 2. The product is butan-2-amine, x = 4, so x + y = 6. The migrating carbon keeps its configuration (retention), and the priority order (N or COOH &gt; ethyl &gt; methyl &gt; H) is unchanged, so the product is (<i>S</i>)-butan-2-amine.")),

 (40, ("(Medium, integer) Adipamide (hexanediamide) is treated with excess Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOH in water. How many moles of NaOH and of Br<sub>2</sub> are consumed per mole of diamide, and what is the organic product? Give (moles NaOH) + (moles Br<sub>2</sub>).",
      None,
      "10 (8 NaOH + 2 Br2); butane-1,4-diamine",
      "Each CONH<sub>2</sub> group follows RCONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> + 4 NaOH → RNH<sub>2</sub> + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2 NaBr + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O (N-bromination, deprotonation, 1,2-shift of R to N with loss of Br<sup>−</sup>, hydrolysis of RNCO, and CO<sub>2</sub> captured as carbonate). Two groups consume 2 Br<sub>2</sub> and 8 NaOH. Each C(=O) is lost, so the C<sub>6</sub> diamide gives the C<sub>4</sub> diamine H<sub>2</sub>N(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (putrescine).")),

 (41, ("(Medium, multi-correct) Choose the correct statement(s): (A) singlet nitrenes add to <i>cis</i>-but-2-ene to give <i>cis</i>-2,3-dimethylaziridines stereospecifically, whereas triplet nitrenes give a mixture of <i>cis</i> and <i>trans</i> aziridines; (B) NH and simple alkyl nitrenes have triplet ground states; (C) free alkyl nitrenes are rarely trapped intermolecularly because the singlet state undergoes very fast 1,2-H shift to an imine; (D) in the thermal Curtius rearrangement of an acyl azide a free acyl nitrene is formed first and R then migrates onto it; (E) nylon-6 is made by ring-opening polymerisation of ε-caprolactam, which itself comes from the Beckmann rearrangement of cyclohexanone oxime; (F) PhI=NTs delivers a free triplet nitrene TsN to a Cu catalyst.",
      None,
      "A, B, C, E",
      "(A) is correct: singlet addition is concerted, triplet addition is stepwise through a 1,3-diradical that can rotate. (B) NH and CH<sub>3</sub>N are triplets in the ground state. (C) Singlet alkylnitrenes rearrange to imines essentially without barrier. (D) is false: thermal Curtius is concerted, R migrates as N<sub>2</sub> leaves, with no free nitrene (photolysis can give some). (E) is correct. (F) is false: Cu or Rh nitrenoids (metal-bound imido species) are the transfer agents, not free nitrenes.")),

 (42, ("(Medium, GATE/CSIR style) Acetophenone oxime exists as an (<i>E</i>) isomer (OH anti to phenyl, major) and a (<i>Z</i>) isomer. Each isomer is treated separately with PCl<sub>5</sub> and then water. Give the amide from each isomer and the two fragments obtained when each amide is hydrolysed with aqueous HCl (heat).",
      S([("(E)-oxime", "C/C(=N\\O)c1ccccc1"), ("(Z)-oxime", "C/C(=N/O)c1ccccc1")]),
      "E: acetanilide → aniline + acetic acid; Z: N-methylbenzamide → methylamine + benzoic acid",
      "In the Beckmann rearrangement the group anti to the departing OH (as O–PCl<sub>4</sub> or protonated OH) migrates to nitrogen, stereospecifically. In the (<i>E</i>) isomer phenyl is anti, so Ph–N results: PhNHCOCH<sub>3</sub>, which hydrolyses to anilinium chloride and acetic acid. In the (<i>Z</i>) isomer methyl is anti: CH<sub>3</sub>NHCOPh, hydrolysing to methylammonium chloride and benzoic acid.")),

 (43, ("(Medium) 2-Methylpentanamide is subjected to: (i) Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOH; (ii) excess CH<sub>3</sub>I; (iii) moist Ag<sub>2</sub>O; (iv) heat. Name the organic products of steps (i) and (iv), say which alkene is major, and state the number of carbon atoms in it. Distinguish the two ‘Hofmann’ reactions involved.",
      None,
      "pentan-2-amine; major pent-1-ene (C5)",
      "Step (i) is the Hofmann <i>rearrangement</i>: C<sub>6</sub> amide → C<sub>5</sub> amine, pentan-2-amine, with loss of the carbonyl carbon as carbonate. Steps (ii)–(iv) are Hofmann <i>exhaustive methylation/elimination</i> of the trimethylammonium hydroxide: E2 with the bulky NMe<sub>3</sub> leaving group removes the most accessible, most acidic β-H (the CH<sub>3</sub> hydrogens), giving pent-1-ene (Hofmann product) as the major alkene, with minor (<i>E</i>)- and (<i>Z</i>)-pent-2-ene and NMe<sub>3</sub>. The two reactions share a name but are unrelated mechanistically.")),

 (44, ("(Medium) Three cyclic imides are each treated with Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH (aq) and then neutralised: phthalimide, succinimide and glutarimide. Give the amino acid obtained from each and the sum of the numbers of carbon atoms in the three products.",
      S([("phthalimide", "O=C1NC(=O)c2ccccc12"), ("succinimide", "O=C1CCC(=O)N1"), ("glutarimide", "O=C1CCCC(=O)N1")]),
      "anthranilic acid (C7), β-alanine (C3), 4-aminobutanoic acid (C4); sum = 14",
      "Hydroxide opens the imide to an amide-carboxylate (phthalamate, succinamate, glutaramate); the free CONH<sub>2</sub> then undergoes Hofmann rearrangement, losing its carbon as carbonate and leaving the carboxylate as the other end. Phthalimide gives 2-aminobenzoic (anthranilic) acid, succinimide gives H<sub>2</sub>N(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>COOH, glutarimide gives H<sub>2</sub>N(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>3</sub>COOH (GABA). Carbons: 7 + 3 + 4 = 14.")),

 (45, ("(Hard) (<i>E</i>)-2-Nitrostilbene is heated at about 160 °C with an excess of triethyl phosphite. (a) Give the organic product. (b) What is the minimum number of equivalents of P(OEt)<sub>3</sub> and what P-containing product forms? (c) Why is the 5-membered ring product formed and not a 3-membered one? (Cadogan–Sundberg indole synthesis.)",
      S([("2-nitrostilbene", "[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1/C=C/c1ccccc1"), ("product", "c1ccc2[nH]c(-c3ccccc3)cc2c1")]),
      "2-phenylindole; 2 equiv P(OEt)3, giving 2 OP(OEt)3 (triethyl phosphate)",
      "Each equivalent of phosphite removes one oxygen: ArNO<sub>2</sub> → ArNO → ArN (nitrene or nitrene-like species), forming 2 O=P(OEt)<sub>3</sub>. The electron-deficient N then bonds to the alkene carbon bearing the phenyl group (5-membered ring: N, two aryl carbons, two alkene carbons); attack on the nearer alkene carbon would give a strained azirine-type 3-ring. A 1,2-H shift (formally) then aromatises to the NH indole, 2-phenylindole. ⚑ Whether a free nitrene or a 2-nitrosoarene/N-hydroxy-type intermediate is the cyclising species is still debated; the product is the same.")),

 (46, ("(Hard) Phenyl azide is irradiated (UV) at room temperature in neat diethylamine. (a) Give the product and its molecular formula. (b) List, in order, the intermediates between singlet phenylnitrene and the product. (c) How many sp<sup>3</sup> carbon atoms does the product contain?",
      S([("phenyl azide", "[N-]=[N+]=Nc1ccccc1"), ("product", "CCN(CC)C1=NC=CC=CC1")]),
      "2-(diethylamino)-3H-azepine, C10H16N2; 5 sp3 carbons",
      "Loss of N<sub>2</sub> gives singlet phenylnitrene, which closes to a benzazirine (fused 2<i>H</i>-azirine), which opens to the strained ketenimine 1-aza-1,2,4,6-cycloheptatetraene (didehydroazepine). Et<sub>2</sub>NH adds to the central ketenimine carbon C-2 and the proton ends on C-3, giving 2-(diethylamino)-3<i>H</i>-azepine, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N + C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>11</sub>N = C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>16</sub>N<sub>2</sub>. sp<sup>3</sup> carbons: ring C-3 (CH<sub>2</sub>) plus four ethyl carbons = 5. Without a trap the ketenimine gives tars; the triplet nitrene (favoured by low temperature or heavy atoms) dimerises to azobenzene.")),

 (47, ("(Hard) (<i>S</i>)-2-Methyl-3-(phenylthio)propanoic acid is converted by diphenylphosphoryl azide (DPPA)/Et<sub>3</sub>N in toluene at 80 °C, followed by <i>t</i>-BuOH, into a Boc carbamate. (a) Does the stereocentre change its spatial arrangement? (b) Give the CIP descriptor of the carbamate and explain any change from the acid.",
      S([("(S)-acid", "C[C@H](CSc1ccccc1)C(=O)O"), ("carbamate", "C[C@H](CSc1ccccc1)NC(=O)OC(C)(C)C")]),
      "No; spatial arrangement retained but descriptor changes S → R",
      "DPPA converts the acid to the acyl azide, which rearranges to the isocyanate; <i>t</i>-BuOH traps it as BocNH–. The migrating carbon moves with full retention (concerted shift), so the stereocentre is not touched. In the acid the priorities are CH<sub>2</sub>SPh (S,H,H) &gt; COOH (O,O,O) &gt; CH<sub>3</sub> &gt; H, because S outranks O at the first point of difference. In the carbamate N outranks C, so N &gt; CH<sub>2</sub>SPh &gt; CH<sub>3</sub> &gt; H. The group that replaced COOH moved from rank 2 to rank 1 and CH<sub>2</sub>SPh from 1 to 2, a swap, so the label changes from (<i>S</i>) to (<i>R</i>) without any inversion.")),

 (48, ("(Hard, match the column) Match each reaction in Column I with the species that undergoes the 1,2-shift (just before the migrating group moves) in Column II. Column I: P Hofmann rearrangement of RCONH<sub>2</sub> with Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOH; Q Curtius rearrangement; R Lossen rearrangement; S Beckmann rearrangement of a ketoxime in H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>; T Schmidt reaction of a ketone with HN<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>. Column II: 1 acyl azide; 2 conjugate base of an N-bromoamide; 3 anion of an O-acyl (or O-sulfonyl) hydroxamic acid; 4 O-protonated oxime; 5 iminodiazonium ion; 6 free nitrene.",
      None,
      "P–2, Q–1, R–3, S–4, T–5 (6 matches none)",
      "Hofmann: the N-bromoamide anion RC(=O)N<sup>−</sup>Br loses Br<sup>−</sup> as R shifts. Curtius: the acyl azide loses N<sub>2</sub> as R shifts. Lossen: the activated hydroxamate RC(=O)N<sup>−</sup>OCOR′ loses carboxylate as R shifts. Beckmann: the O-protonated oxime loses H<sub>2</sub>O as the anti group shifts. Schmidt: the azidohydrin dehydrates to the iminodiazonium ion R<sub>2</sub>C=N–N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>, which loses N<sub>2</sub> as R shifts. In all five the shift is concerted with loss of the leaving group, so a free nitrene (6) is not involved.")),

 (49, ("(Hard) (<i>S</i>)-2-Methylcyclohexanone gives an oxime in which the OH is anti to C-2 (the more substituted α-carbon, the (<i>E</i>) oxime). This is treated with PCl<sub>5</sub>, then water. (a) Give the lactam, with ring size and descriptor. (b) What lactam would the isomer with OH syn to C-2 give? (c) What is the repeating unit of the polymer made by ring-opening polymerisation of the parent unsubstituted lactam from cyclohexanone oxime?",
      S([("(S)-ketone", "C[C@H]1CCCCC1=O"), ("lactam", "C[C@H]1CCCCC(=O)N1")]),
      "(S)-7-methylazepan-2-one; 3-methylazepan-2-one; nylon-6 unit –[NH(CH2)5CO]–",
      "The group anti to OH migrates, so with OH anti to C-2 the substituted C-2 moves from C-1 to N. N is then bonded to C(CH<sub>3</sub>) and the carbonyl carbon (old C-1) to C-6, giving a seven-membered lactam, 7-methylazepan-2-one. The stereocentre C-2 is the migrating carbon and keeps its configuration; the priority order (C-1 or N &gt; C-3 &gt; CH<sub>3</sub> &gt; H) is unchanged, so (<i>S</i>) stays (<i>S</i>). The other oxime isomer would shift C-6 instead, putting the methyl α to the carbonyl: 3-methylazepan-2-one. Unsubstituted ε-caprolactam polymerises to nylon-6, repeating unit –NH(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>5</sub>C(=O)–.")),

 (50, ("(Hard) The hydroxylamine (4-nitrophenyl)diphenylmethyl–NHOH is treated with PCl<sub>5</sub>, and the product is then hydrolysed with aqueous acid. (a) Give the intermediate imine (Stieglitz rearrangement). (b) Give the two final organic products. (c) Explain which aryl group migrates.",
      S([("substrate", "O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(cc1)C(NO)(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1")]),
      "imine Ph(4-O2NC6H4)C=N-Ph (N-phenyl imine); then 4-nitrobenzophenone + aniline",
      "Activation of the N–O bond (O–PCl<sub>4</sub>, leaving group) triggers a 1,2-shift of an aryl group from the trityl carbon to the electron-deficient nitrogen, as in Hofmann/Curtius; the carbon is left as an iminium ion (R<sub>2</sub>C=N<sup>+</sup>HAr after loss of the N–H proton, giving the neutral N-aryl ketimine). Phenyl migrates in preference to the strongly electron-poor 4-nitrophenyl (a poor migrating group in cationic shifts), so N bears phenyl and the C=N carbon keeps one phenyl and the nitrophenyl. Hydrolysis gives 4-nitrobenzophenone and anilinium ion. ⚑ Relative migratory aptitudes of aryl groups here are electronic and assumed from the cationic-shift trend.")),

 (51, ("(Hard) N-Phenylhydroxylamine is warmed in (i) dilute aqueous H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> and (ii) H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> in methanol (Bamberger rearrangement). Give the main product in each case, identify the key electrophilic intermediate, and state at how many atoms (N plus ring carbons) the positive charge of that intermediate is delocalised in the resonance picture.",
      S([("PhNHOH", "ONc1ccccc1")]),
      "(i) 4-aminophenol; (ii) 4-methoxyaniline; phenylnitrenium ion, charge on 4 atoms",
      "Protonation on O and loss of water (a formal N–O heterolysis) gives the phenylnitrenium ion PhNH<sup>+</sup>, an electron-sextet nitrogen cation that is stabilised by the ring: positive charge on N and on the two ortho and the para carbons, 4 atoms in all. The nucleophile (H<sub>2</sub>O or CH<sub>3</sub>OH, the only nucleophile in the medium) attacks para, and tautomerisation restores aromaticity: 4-aminophenol in water, 4-methoxyaniline in methanol. ⚑ Whether a discrete nitrenium ion or a tight ion pair is involved is system-dependent; the para selectivity is the same.")),

 (52, ("(Hard, assertion–reason) Choose for each pair: (A) both true and R explains A; (B) both true but R does not explain A; (C) A true, R false; (D) A false, R true. <b>Pair 1.</b> A: Insertion of a singlet alkylnitrene into the tertiary C–H bond of an optically active alkane occurs with retention of configuration. R: Singlet insertion is a concerted three-centre process that does not generate a free radical pair. <b>Pair 2.</b> A: The Hofmann rearrangement of an optically active α-chiral primary amide gives the amine with retention. R: The migrating carbon moves with its bonding pair while Br<sup>−</sup> leaves, without becoming a free carbocation. <b>Pair 3.</b> A: Thermal decomposition of an acyl azide at 80 °C proceeds through a free acyl nitrene, so C–H insertion and aziridination by-products are formed. R: Photolysis of acyl azides can give acyl nitrenes.",
      None,
      "Pair 1: A; Pair 2: A; Pair 3: D",
      "Pair 1: singlet insertion proceeds through a concerted three-centre transition state with no radical pair, so the migrating configuration is retained; R explains A (a triplet nitrene would abstract H and recombine, giving racemisation). Pair 2: a concerted 1,2-shift with simultaneous loss of the leaving group on N keeps the configuration of the migrating carbon; R explains A. Pair 3: A is false, since thermal Curtius rearrangement is concerted and gives isocyanate cleanly without nitrene by-products; R is true, as acyl nitrenes are formed photochemically, so the answer is D.")),

 (53, ("(Hard) Cyclohexene is treated with PhI=NTs and a Cu(I) catalyst to give aziridine <b>A</b>. <b>A</b> is heated with NaN<sub>3</sub> in aqueous DMF to give <b>B</b>, and B is hydrogenated (H<sub>2</sub>, Pd/C) to <b>C</b>. (a) Identify A, B, C. (b) State the relative configuration in B and C and the number of stereoisomers formed. (c) Is a free nitrene involved in the first step?",
      S([("A", "Cc1ccc(cc1)S(=O)(=O)N1C2CCCCC12"), ("B", "Cc1ccc(cc1)S(=O)(=O)NC1CCCCC1N=[N+]=[N-]")]),
      "A cis-fused N-tosylaziridine; B trans-azido sulfonamide; C trans-N-tosyl-1,2-diaminocyclohexane (racemic, 2 stereoisomers); no free nitrene",
      "The Cu-bound iminoiodinane gives a Cu-nitrenoid (nitrenoid, not a free nitrene) that transfers NTs to the alkene, giving the cis-fused 7-azabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane A (a bicyclic aziridine can only be cis). Azide opens the activated aziridine by SN2 with inversion at the carbon attacked, so the N and the new N<sub>3</sub> are trans on the ring: B is trans-N-(2-azidocyclohexyl)-4-methylbenzenesulfonamide. Catalytic hydrogenation reduces N<sub>3</sub> to NH<sub>2</sub> without touching stereochemistry, giving trans-TsNH–C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>10</sub>–NH<sub>2</sub>. Since no chiral catalyst or auxiliary is used, the (1<i>R</i>,2<i>R</i>) and (1<i>S</i>,2<i>S</i>) forms arise equally: a racemate (2 stereoisomers).")),

 (54, ("(Hard) (<i>E</i>)-Cinnamamide, PhCH=CH–CONH<sub>2</sub>, is subjected to (i) NaOCl, aqueous NaOH, and separately (ii) Br<sub>2</sub>, NaOMe, MeOH. Give the main product in each case, with carbon count, and explain why they differ.",
      None,
      "(i) phenylacetaldehyde (C8); (ii) methyl (E)-styrylcarbamate, PhCH=CH–NHCO2CH3 (C10)",
      "Both reactions give the vinyl isocyanate (<i>E</i>)-PhCH=CH–N=C=O by Hofmann rearrangement; the vinyl carbon migrates with retention of the (<i>E</i>) geometry ⚑ (stereospecific for sp<sup>2</sup> migration). In water the isocyanate is hydrolysed to the unstable carbamic acid, which loses CO<sub>2</sub> to give the enamine PhCH=CH–NH<sub>2</sub>, whose tautomer PhCH<sub>2</sub>CH=NH hydrolyses to phenylacetaldehyde, C<sub>8</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O (loss of one carbon from the C<sub>9</sub> amide). In methanol the isocyanate is trapped faster as the enecarbamate methyl (<i>E</i>)-N-styrylcarbamate, which is stable.")),

 (55, ("(Very hard) Design a short conversion of cyclohexanone into hexa-1,5-diene that uses a Beckmann rearrangement and two Hofmann eliminations. Give the reagents and every intermediate, count the total equivalents of CH<sub>3</sub>I consumed, and name the nitrogen-containing by-product of the last step. Why can this sequence only give the terminal alkene?",
      S([("ketone", "O=C1CCCCC1"), ("lactam", "O=C1CCCCCN1"), ("azepane", "C1CCCNCC1"), ("diene", "C=CCCC=C")]),
      "oxime; caprolactam; azepane; N,N-dimethylhex-5-en-1-amine; hexa-1,5-diene; 3 CH3I; NMe3",
      "(1) NH<sub>2</sub>OH gives the oxime; (2) H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>/oleum (Beckmann) gives ε-caprolactam (azepan-2-one); (3) LiAlH<sub>4</sub> reduces the lactam to azepane; (4) excess CH<sub>3</sub>I (2 equiv), then Ag<sub>2</sub>O/H<sub>2</sub>O and heat: the ring N<sup>+</sup>Me<sub>2</sub> hydroxide undergoes Hofmann elimination to give Me<sub>2</sub>N(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub>; (5) 1 more equiv of CH<sub>3</sub>I, Ag<sub>2</sub>O, heat: the acyclic Me<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup> eliminates with the only β-H set (C-2), giving CH<sub>2</sub>=CH(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> and NMe<sub>3</sub>. Total 3 CH<sub>3</sub>I. Both eliminations give terminal C=C: in the azepanium ion the two N-bearing carbons are equivalent by symmetry and each has one β-carbon, and in the second step the Me<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-bearing C-1 has only C-2 as β-carbon.")),

 (56, ("(Very hard) 3-Methylbutyl sulfamate, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CHCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OSO<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, is treated with Rh<sub>2</sub>(OAc)<sub>4</sub> (cat.), PhI(OAc)<sub>2</sub> and MgO in CH<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub> to give <b>P</b>. <b>P</b> is then treated with NaN<sub>3</sub> in DMF, followed by aqueous acid, to give <b>Q</b>, and finally H<sub>2</sub>/Pd gives <b>R</b>. Identify P, Q, R, state the ring size of P and the type of reactive species, and explain the site of ring opening.",
      S([("sulfamate", "CC(C)CCOS(=O)(=O)N"), ("P", "CC1(C)CCOS(=O)(=O)N1")]),
      "P 4,4-dimethyl-1,2,3-oxathiazinane 2,2-dioxide (6-ring); Q 4-azido-2-methylbutan-2-amine; R 3-methylbutane-1,3-diamine",
      "PhI(OAc)<sub>2</sub> and the sulfamate generate an iminoiodinane in situ that reacts with Rh<sub>2</sub>(OAc)<sub>4</sub> to give a Rh-nitrenoid (not a free nitrene). Its geometry prefers a six-membered chair-like transition state, so it inserts into the γ-C–H, here the weak tertiary C–H, forming the six-membered cyclic sulfamate P: O–SO<sub>2</sub>–N–C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>–CH<sub>2</sub>–CH<sub>2</sub>. Azide attacks by SN2 at the primary O–CH<sub>2</sub> carbon (the quaternary C–N carbon cannot undergo SN2); the N-sulfate that results is hydrolysed in acid to NH<sub>2</sub>, giving Q = N<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>. Hydrogenation of the azide gives the 1,3-diamine R, H<sub>2</sub>NCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>.")),

 (57, ("(Very hard) (<i>S</i>)-2-Methylbutanoic acid is converted to a single amine by: (i) SOCl<sub>2</sub>; (ii) excess CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub>; (iii) Ag<sub>2</sub>O, H<sub>2</sub>O (Wolff rearrangement) to give acid <b>X</b>; (iv) DPPA, Et<sub>3</sub>N, then H<sub>2</sub>O/H<sup>+</sup> (Curtius). (a) Give X and the final amine, with carbon counts. (b) State the CIP descriptors of X and the amine and the configuration changes at the stereocentre. (c) Compare with the amine from direct Curtius of the starting acid.",
      S([("start", "CC[C@H](C)C(=O)O"), ("X", "CC[C@H](C)CC(=O)O"), ("amine", "CC[C@H](C)CN")]),
      "X = (S)-3-methylpentanoic acid (C6); amine = (S)-2-methylbutan-1-amine (C5); direct Curtius gives (S)-butan-2-amine (C4)",
      "Arndt–Eistert homologation adds one CH<sub>2</sub> between the stereocentre and COOH: the Wolff shift occurs at the carbonyl-attached carbon (the stereocentre) with retention, so X is (<i>S</i>)-3-methylpentanoic acid, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. The CH<sub>2</sub>COOH group still outranks ethyl (second sphere O,O,O versus H,H,H), so the descriptor stays (<i>S</i>). Curtius then removes the COOH carbon, with retention at the migrating CH<sub>2</sub> (not the stereocentre), giving (<i>S</i>)-2-methylbutan-1-amine, C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>13</sub>N, in which CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> outranks ethyl. Direct Curtius of the starting acid instead would give the C<sub>4</sub> amine (<i>S</i>)-butan-2-amine. Net: homologation followed by degradation puts a CH<sub>2</sub> between the stereocentre and N.")),

 (58, ("(Very hard, counting) Ethoxycarbonylnitrene (singlet, from photolysis of ethyl azidoformate) is generated in 2-methylbutane as solvent and inserts into C–H bonds to give N-alkyl carbamates EtO<sub>2</sub>C–NH–R. How many different constitutional isomers of the carbamate are possible, and how many stereoisomers (counting each enantiomer separately, all isomers) can form in total? Which isomer is expected to be the major product?",
      S([("a", "CCOC(=O)NC(C)(C)CC"), ("b", "CCOC(=O)NC(C)C(C)C"), ("c", "CCOC(=O)NCC(C)CC"), ("d", "CCOC(=O)NCCC(C)C")]),
      "4 constitutional isomers; 6 stereoisomers; major: ethyl N-(2-methylbutan-2-yl)carbamate",
      "2-Methylbutane, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH–CH<sub>2</sub>–CH<sub>3</sub>, has four types of C–H: the two equivalent CH<sub>3</sub> on C-2, the C-2 methine, the C-3 methylene and the C-4 CH<sub>3</sub>. Hence 4 constitutional carbamates (a–d). Inserting at C-3 gives CH<sub>3</sub>CH(NHR)CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>, with a stereocentre (2 enantiomers); inserting at one of the C-2 methyls gives NHCH<sub>2</sub>CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>, also chiral (2 enantiomers); the tertiary insertion and the C-4 insertion products are achiral (1 each). Total 2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 6. The weak tertiary C–H is the most reactive site per hydrogen, so isomer a is the main product. ⚑ The selectivity of singlet carbonylnitrene insertion is modest, so a is the major but not an exclusive product.")),

 (59, ("(Very hard, structure elucidation) Compound <b>K</b>, C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O, shows IR 1688 cm<sup>−1</sup> and <sup>1</sup>H NMR δ 1.22 (t, 3H), 2.99 (q, 2H), 7.4–7.6 (m, 3H), 7.95 (d, 2H). K with NH<sub>2</sub>OH gives a single major oxime <b>A</b>. A with PCl<sub>5</sub>, then water, gives <b>B</b>, C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>11</sub>NO, IR 3300 and 1660 cm<sup>−1</sup>, <sup>1</sup>H NMR δ 1.2 (t, 3H), 2.4 (q, 2H), 7.0–7.6 (m, 5H), 7.9 (br s, 1H). Hot 6 M HCl converts B into <b>C</b> (an anilinium salt) and <b>D</b>. Identify K, A, B, C, D and state the number of distinct <sup>13</sup>C signals of B. Why is the isomeric N-ethylbenzamide ruled out?",
      S([("K", "CCC(=O)c1ccccc1"), ("B", "CCC(=O)Nc1ccccc1")]),
      "K propiophenone; A (E)-oxime; B N-phenylpropanamide; C anilinium chloride; D propanoic acid; 7 13C signals",
      "The ketone C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O with ethyl (t 1.22, q 2.99; the CH<sub>2</sub> is deshielded by C=O) and a monosubstituted conjugated aryl ketone is propiophenone, K. The ketoxime A is mainly (<i>E</i>) with OH anti to the bulky phenyl, so Beckmann rearrangement moves phenyl to N: B is PhNHC(=O)CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>. In B the CH<sub>2</sub> is at δ 2.4 (next to C=O, typical of an amide), and there is no deshielded N–CH<sub>2</sub> (a CH<sub>2</sub> attached to N, as in N-ethylbenzamide, would be near δ 3.4 (a quintet-like multiplet) with an NH at ≈ 6.2 and ortho H at 7.8 d). Hydrolysis gives anilinium chloride (C) and propanoic acid (D). 13C signals of B: CH<sub>3</sub>, CH<sub>2</sub>, C=O, and four aromatic (ipso, ortho, meta, para) = 7.")),

 (60, ("(Very hard) The trans-2-phenylcyclopropanecarboxylic acid with (1<i>S</i>,2<i>S</i>) configuration is converted by (i) ClCO<sub>2</sub>Et, Et<sub>3</sub>N; (ii) NaN<sub>3</sub>; (iii) toluene, reflux; (iv) aqueous HCl, heat; (v) NaOH to trans-2-phenylcyclopropylamine (tranylcypromine). (a) Give the CIP descriptors of the amine and the relative configuration. (b) Explain why a descriptor changes even though the reaction proceeds with retention at the migrating carbon. (c) Why can this amine not be made by SN2 from the corresponding bromide?",
      S([("(1S,2S)-acid", "OC(=O)[C@H]1C[C@@H]1c1ccccc1"), ("amine", "N[C@H]1C[C@@H]1c1ccccc1")]),
      "(1S,2R)-trans-2-phenylcyclopropylamine; C-2 descriptor flips S → R though geometry is retained",
      "Mixed anhydride, acyl azide, Curtius (isocyanate, N<sub>2</sub> lost), then hydrolysis and loss of CO<sub>2</sub> give the amine; the cyclopropyl carbon C-1 migrates with retention, so the trans relationship persists. At C-1 the priorities N (replacing COOH) &gt; C-2 &gt; C-3 &gt; H keep the same order, so C-1 remains (<i>S</i>). At C-2 the substituents are Ph (C,C,C), C-1 and C-3: in the acid C-1 is (C,C,H), below Ph, but in the amine C-1 carries N, so C-1 is (N,C,H), which outranks Ph (C,C,C) at the first point of difference. The ranks of Ph and C-1 are swapped, so C-2 changes from (<i>S</i>) to (<i>R</i>) with no change in geometry. The amine is (1<i>S</i>,2<i>R</i>), which is still trans. A cyclopropyl halide cannot undergo SN2 (backside attack in a 3-ring is prohibited by geometry) and its SN1 pathway would give a very unstable cyclopropyl cation, so the carboxylic acid route is used.")),
]
